card.statement:
Let $\mathbb F$ be a field or skew field, $V$ be a right vector space over $\mathbb F$, and $U$ be its subspace. Let ${\cal A}$ be one of linear mappings

\begin{equation}
U\rightarrow V,\qquad
V\rightarrow U,\qquad
V/U\rightarrow V,\qquad
V\rightarrow V/U.
\end{equation}

(a) There exists a basis $e_1,\dots,e_n$ of\/ $V$ satisfying <<ref:m.2>>, in which for the matrix $A_e$ of ${\cal
A}$ we have:

\[
\begin{cases}
A_e^T,
&\text{if $U\to V$ or $V/U\to
V$,}
\\
A_e, &\text{if $V\to U$ or
$V\to V/U$}
\end{cases}
\]

is a block-direct sum of a $p$-by-$p$ matrix

\begin{equation}
[K|0_{p0}],\qquad \text{$K$ is
nonsingular,}
\end{equation}

and matrices of the form

\begin{equation}
[J_q(0)|0_{q0}],
\qquad
[J_q(0)|E_q],
\end{equation}

where $E_q$ was defined in <<ref:bip1s>> $($the summands <<ref:m.5>> or <<ref:m.6>> may be absent$)$. The block $K$ is determined by ${\cal A}$ uniquely up to similarity, and the summands of the form <<ref:m.6>> are determined by ${\cal A}$ uniquely up to permutation.

(b) If\/ $\mathbb F=\mathbb C$, then one can replace the summand <<ref:m.5>> by a block-direct sum of square matrices of the form

\begin{equation*}
[J_q(\lambda)|0_{q0}].
\end{equation*}

The obtained matrix is determined by ${\cal A}$ uniquely up to permutation of summands, and so it is a canonical matrix of the linear mapping ${\cal A}$.
